{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Behold, the people of the children of Israel are more numerous and mightier than us.</b> He added the word ״עַם״, for he should have said merely ״הִנֵּה בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Also, what is written ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל עַמּוֹ״ (Exodus 1:9) — did he then speak to the entire people? He should have said ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל שָׂרָיו וַחֲכָמָיו״. Rather, this is what Pharaoh said to them: it is not possible for two opposing peoples to dwell in a single land. Therefore it says ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶל עַמּוֹ״ — for the sake of his people, to say: You are a distinctive people in the land, and behold — the people of the children of Israel: for the children of Israel too, lo, they have become a people as well; in a brief time they became a numerous people. Even though they are not yet more numerous than us, nevertheless they are mightier than us, strong by nature. For the way of all twins is to be weak by nature, and here six were born in a single womb and even so they are stronger by nature than us. Therefore ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה לוֹ פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:10) — lest, added to their strength by nature, they also become more numerous than us. And even though in the word ״יִרְבֶּה״ it is not yet explicit that they were more numerous than them, nevertheless he went back and explained his words afterward and said ״וְהָיָה כִּי תִקְרֶאנָה מִלְחָמָה וְנוֹסַף גַּם הוּא עַל שֹׂנְאֵינוּ״ (Exodus 1:10) — as if he had said ״וְנוֹסַף גַּם הוּא עָלֵינוּ״. That is to say, they are already mightier than us, and if they multiply further, then they will also be added over us. And the word ״גַּם״ refers to the might. And what he said ״עַל שֹׂנְאֵינוּ״ — like one who hangs his curse upon his enemies, he said that they would be numerous upon our enemies. And his main intention was to say that they would be numerous upon us, but he did not wish to say ״עָלֵינוּ״ so as to hang the curse upon himself, so he hung it upon his enemies, saying they would be numerous and added upon our enemies. Although regarding the matter of might he did hang it upon himself and said ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״, because that was already so; but the multiplication that was yet to come, he did not wish to hang upon himself." "And since our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Exodus Rabbah 1:9) ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה לוֹ״ — to their savior, that is, to the savior of Israel, it appears proper to explain that the word ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ also refers to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the manner that they interpreted (Sotah 35a) the verse ״כִּי חָזָק הוּא מִמֶּנּוּ״ (Numbers 13:31) — as it were, even the master of the house cannot remove his vessels from there. So too ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״ is referred to the Holy One, blessed be He, and the nun of ״מִמֶּנּוּ״ is with a cholam, meaning that all the strength of Israel is drawn to them from Him, blessed be He — that is, from the source of Israel. Therefore ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה לוֹ״ — to their source, that is, to their savior." "Or one may say ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״ literally. For they are mightier than Him, blessed be He, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Mo'ed Katan 16b): the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I rule over all — and who rules over Me? The righteous person, as it is written (II Samuel 23:3) ״צַדִּיק מֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹהִים״ — that I decree a decree and the righteous person annuls it. And assuredly the essence of their strength and might of their hand is because they are cleaving to His commandments and Torahs, to observe them. Therefore ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה לוֹ״ — to create a breach among those who cleave, for by making them evil and sinful toward HaShem, their strength would depart — as the commentators explained on the verse ״וַיָּרֵעוּ אֹתָנוּ הַמִּצְרִים וַיְעַנּוּנוּ״ (Deuteronomy 26:6), for he should have said ״וַיָּרֵעוּ לָנוּ הַמִּצְרִים״; rather he used ״אֹתָנוּ״ to say that the Egyptians made us evil and sinful toward HaShem, and through this ״וַיְעַנּוּנוּ״ — and they afflicted us." "And behold, the words of the commentators are true, that Israel in Egypt were evil and sinful, as it is written in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 20:5–7) ״וָאִוָּדַע לָהֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ and so forth, ״אִישׁ שִׁקּוּצֵי עֵינָיו הִשְׁלִיכוּ״ and so forth. But in the Torah we find no hint of this at the time of the event. Therefore it appears to me that this verse that says ״הָבָה נִתְחַכְּמָה לוֹ״ — that is, by their stratagems through which they schemed to make them sin. ״וַיָּשִׂימוּ עָלָיו שָׂרֵי מִסִּים״ — to impose a tax for the overseers of idolatry, for this is like as if they actually served idolatry, as Rashi explained in parashat Ki Tavo on the verse ״וַעֲבַדְתָּם שָׁם אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים״ (Deuteronomy 28:64). Therefore it says ״לְמַעַן עַנֹּתוֹ בְּסִבְלֹתָם״, exactly like what is said ״וַיָּרֵעוּ אֹתָנוּ הַמִּצְרִים וַיְעַנּוּנוּ״ (Deuteronomy 26:6) — that by making us evil and sinful they found an opening for ״וַיְעַנּוּנוּ״, for then they had power over us. So too he said here ״וַיָּשִׂימוּ עָלָיו שָׂרֵי מִסִּים״ to give a tax to idolatry ״לְמַעַן עַנֹּתוֹ״, for it follows from this that they would be able to afflict them with their burdens, once their shade departs from above them when they turn away from following HaShem.\nAnd perhaps it is concerning this that it says ״וַיַּעֲבִידוּ מִצְרַיִם אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּפָרֶךְ״, for this entire verse appears superfluous, and it may be that he alluded by this that through imposing officers of labor over them, it is as if they enslaved them to idolatry. Therefore it says ״וַיַּעֲבִידוּ מִצְרַיִם אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּפָרֶךְ״ — that they enslaved them to idolatry first with a soft mouth, through incitement and enticement, and afterward בְּפָרֶךְ to actual labor through coercion. Therefore ״וַיְמָרְרוּ אֶת חַיֵּיהֶם״, for concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, it is written (Deuteronomy 30:20) ״כִּי הוּא חַיֶּיךָ״ and so forth, and by turning away from HaShem they embittered their lives ״בַּעֲבֹדָה קָשָׁה״ — that is, in idolatry, which is harsh and evil. And afterward ״בְּחֹמֶר וּבִלְבֵנִים וּבְכָל עֲבֹדָה בַּשָּׂדֶה״ — behold, unspecified labor in the field is mentioned, and afterward he said ״אֵת כָּל עֲבֹדָתָם״, that is, also labor that is in the city. And how do I fulfill ״וַיְמָרְרוּ אֶת חַיֵּיהֶם בַּעֲבֹדָה קָשָׁה״? Rather, he alluded that they defiled them with their idols through idolatrous worship that is harsh, as stated. And because they were included among those who cause the multitude to sin, who are not given the opportunity to repent, therefore HaShem hardened the heart of Pharaoh and his servants. And through this the doubt was resolved in which the early ones struggled concerning the hardening of Pharaoh's heart."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {3 "The verse fragment ״וַיְמָרְרוּ אֶת חַיֵּיהֶ״ is typically found in Exodus 1:14, though the full phrase there reads ״וַיְמָרְרוּ אֶת חַיֵּיהֶם בַּעֲבֹדָה קָשָׁה״; the author quotes it in slightly varying forms throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:13.482198950Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}